{
	title: 'Whole tool output kept; read_blob reads it back',
	spec: 'A tool result over its cap is never lost. When bash (foreground or background) or any tool cuts its output, the host stores the whole output as a text blob of the session (host/blobs.ts, sessions/<id>/blobs/) and the cut result ends with one line naming it, e.g. "[cut: 1.2 MB total, whole output in blob k3x9qa-out; read_blob or cat <path>]". The cut keeps the head and the tail (not only the head), since errors and summaries sit at the end. Memory stays bounded: bash keeps a command's output whole up to jobs.keepChars() (32M characters); past that only its first and last halves stay, the middle counted in a line "[N characters dropped: ...]", so an endless command (`yes` in the background) never exhausts the host. A `read_blob` model tool returns a stored blob by id: "<blob>" in the current session or "<sessionId>/<blob>" in another; ids are checked against their fixed form before naming a file (as blobs.ts already does) so a model-given id never becomes an arbitrary path. Text comes back capped at 1 MB with a cut note; an image blob comes back as an image part. read_blob also accepts a block id as the transcript shows it (#35, or <sessionId>#35) and returns that history block in full: a tool call with its input, a tool result, thinking, or text. An unknown id is an error result. Artifacts old-read-blob.ts, old-blob-ref.ts (reference). Tests: a huge bash output keeps head and tail and names a blob holding it all; an endless command keeps both ends within keepChars and counts what it dropped; read_blob round-trips text and an image; a bad id and a traversal attempt are refused; a block id returns its block.',
	status: 'done',
	needs: ['sj', '1r'],
	notes: [
		'The existing attachments store limits uploads to 5 MB, so tool output uses separate owner-only text blobs under the same validated session blob IDs. Tool previews retain both ends; foreground and background bash no longer discard output before capping. Numbered references use durable history record numbers.'
	]
}
